In India, the Indian Evidence Act, 1872 governs the admission of evidence in courts. According to Section 65 of the Evidence Act, any electronic record, which is printed or written on any material, can be admitted as evidence in court, provided that the conditions mentioned in the section are fulfilled. One of the conditions is that the electronic record should be produced in the court through a certificate which describes:

(a) the particulars of the electronic record;
(b) the manner in which the electronic record was produced;
(c) the particulars of any device involved in the production of that electronic record;
(d) the name, address and other particulars of the person having custody of the device;
(e) the particulars of any relevant conditions or circumstances surrounding the production of the electronic record.

In the case of a recording, the certificate should also mention the time and date of the recording.

Regarding your question, if you have a 10-minute recording and you only want to provide the first 5 minutes as evidence, it is possible to do so. However, you would need to provide a certificate as mentioned above, which describes the particulars of the recording, the manner in which it was produced, and the time and date of the recording. You would also need to edit the recording to include only the relevant 5 minutes and ensure that the edited recording is true and accurate, without any tampering or manipulation.

It is important to note that the court may still consider the entire recording and may not limit its consideration to the edited portion. The court may consider the context and circumstances surrounding the recording, including the conversation that took place before and after the relevant 5 minutes. Therefore, it is advisable to provide the entire recording to the court, along with a clear and specific explanation of why the relevant 5 minutes should be considered as evidence.

In conclusion, while it is possible to provide an edited recording as evidence in court, it is important to follow the procedures laid down in the Indian Evidence Act, 1872 and to ensure that the edited recording is true and accurate. It is also advisable to provide the entire recording to the court, along with a clear and specific explanation of why the relevant portion should be considered as evidence.
